      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly! In your results section, you can interpret these values as follows: The p-value of 0.042 indicates that there is a statistically significant relationship between your independent and dependent variables at the conventional alpha level of 0.05. This means that the likelihood of observing such an effect by chance alone is less than 5%, which is a threshold often used in social sciences to determine statistical significance. The 95% confidence interval for the effect size, ranging from 0.15 to 0.85, suggests that you can be 95% confident that the true effect size lies within this interval. This interval provides a range of plausible values for the effect size, indicating that the relationship is not only statistically significant but also practically meaningful, as the effect size is not trivial. In other words, your findings are robust and suggest a meaningful impact of your independent variable on the dependent variable, with a degree of certainty that is typically required in academic research. Remember, while the p-value tells us about the significance, the confidence interval gives us a sense of the magnitude and precision of the effect size. So, in your paper, you can confidently state that your results are statistically significant and that the effect size is likely to be between 0",
